{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: sampler accepts a one-row diff in ANY row — the status row can win the bet the assert then rejects**

`_oneRowTickDiffPair` returns on the *first* consecutive-frame pair that differs in exactly one row — whichever row that is. The busy window ticks at 1 Hz in (at least) two places: the `· submit` busy row and the status row's ` · turn Ns` seconds cell. A sample that catches only the status row ticking returns that pair, and the contract assert below (`expect(busyA, contains('· submit'))`) then fails — a false "contract violation" that is really a resample-worthy pair. (The old fixed-sample shape had the same exposure once; the new sampler actively *searches*, so it will find such pairs more often.)

Moving the busy-row requirement into the acceptance predicate makes the retry predicate identical to the contract being asserted and removes the false-fail class:

```dart
final changedRows = [
  for (var i = 0; i < gridA.length && i < gridB.length; i++)
    if (gridA[i] != gridB[i]) i,
];
if (gridA.length == gridB.length &&
    changedRows.length == 1 &&
    gridA[changedRows.single].contains('· submit')) {
  return (gridA, gridB);
}
```

The `masked()` content check (only spinner glyph + seconds may move) can then stay where it is, or move into the sampler too — either way the in-body `busyIdx >= 0` loop (line 143) becomes fully redundant and can go.
